          "title": "You'll want to know: мука",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "мука (muká) — \"flour\"."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "мука (muká), \"flour\", stressed on the last syllable from Proto-Slavic mǫka — inherited, from the root of мягкий, \"soft\". Vasmer relates it to Lithuanian minkyti, \"to knead\", and Greek mássō, \"I knead\". мука (muká), stressed on the first syllable, \"torment\", is a related word."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for this chapter."
